#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048229 :topic/id #uuid "8a462f76-1e1a-5c54-833e-8d3d6162407f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054500 :question/id #uuid "04b471e2-edcd-5f01-8677-e93ddc547a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-18927" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} &= \\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt 3 + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be393dbe-03cc-5259-ba28-8f865537c57a" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054501 :question/id #uuid "0e811568-769f-5e18-9c6c-5594c18330b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18931" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}} &= \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{10-6} \\\\ &= \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae5693c-a37e-58f4-8793-0875ef8e0fb8" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Surds"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054502 :question/id #uuid "5a664cc0-30ca-5097-9505-0755963b0f1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18932" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1} &= \\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} \\\\ &= \\frac{(2-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2}-1)}{(\\sqrt{2}+1)(\\sqrt{2}-1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(1)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-2-2+\\sqrt{2}}{2-1} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-4+\\sqrt{2}}{1} \\\\ &= 3\\sqrt{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "203f17e9-3efb-5637-ac02-2c31e5a8a632" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Surds in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :topic/math-academy-id 86 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050180}] :topic/title "Rationalizing Denominators With Two Terms"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847 :topic/id #uuid "18636612-788d-519b-97ca-16774367f5ac" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063991 :question/id #uuid "70f19ea4-d0b8-537a-8ae4-06a236cf37cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-14318" :question/problem "Expand $(4+3y)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=3y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4 + 3y)^2 &= (4)^2 + 2(4) ( 3y) + (3y)^2 \\\\ &= 16 + 24y + 9y^2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288c08af-0583-5588-b767-4254544cf70c" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Linear Binomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063992 :question/id #uuid "c1a6df9e-b1e8-51c8-b5df-4cc624e14193" :question/math-academy-id "e-14319" :question/problem "Expand $(2n^2 + 3)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2n^2$ and $b=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2n^2 + 3)^2 &= (2n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) ( 3) + (3)^2 \\\\[2pt] &= 2^2\\cdot (n^2)^2 + 2(2n^2) (3) + (3)^2 \\\\[2pt] &= 4n^4 + 12n^2 + 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63291c46-6a0f-5e7c-ab0a-acba80fe58f3" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Nonlinear Binomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063993 :question/id #uuid "487f1c4c-63c9-51cf-9a0e-7d9bc0a4c7c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14320" :question/problem "Expand $(3p - 5)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a-b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3p$ and $b=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p - 5)^2 &= (3p)^2 - 2(3p)(5) + 5^2 \\\\ &= 9p^2 - 30p + 25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "afd30bc7-dce9-5e03-a213-2b9695e39091" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring Binomials With Subtraction"}] :topic/math-academy-id 360 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050840}] :topic/title "Squaring Binomials"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848 :topic/id #uuid "6b88d999-b59b-5a39-b090-40b21de4e2a4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184141 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184051 :question/id #uuid "d30aa8b4-820f-5ed1-8878-c5566805e3cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3491" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sin^2\\left(150^\\circ\\right) + 2\\csc^2\\left(240^\\circ\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $150^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $150^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 150^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Now, let's consider the angle $240^\\circ$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $240^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 240^\\circ = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\csc{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc 240^{∘} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin 240^{∘}} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} (150^{∘}) + 2\\csc^{2} (240^{∘}) &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + 2\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{8}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9355cf8a-c924-5d4b-a4c3-fe539bf34faf" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Special Angles in Degrees"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184205 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184106 :question/id #uuid "b0506d9d-2495-505f-b61f-6c01607ad43f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9815" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle 4\\cos^2 \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 6\\right) - \\sin \\left(\\dfrac \\pi 4\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {6}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\dfrac \\pi 4$. From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\dfrac \\pi 4$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).\n$$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\sin\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\cos^{2} \\left(\\frac{π}{6}\\right) - \\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 3 - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "298d3ee7-bb43-5835-8dc5-3306d1f9841f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Special Angles in Radians"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3766 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050938}] :topic/title "Evaluating Trigonometric Expressions"}]}
